WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:05.900
There's so many Queen easter eggs and crucial
details hidden in Bohemian Rhapsody that it's

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.780
guaranteed to blow your mind!

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.920
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video, I'm revealing all the crazy little

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.020
things even some hardcore Queen fans might
have missed in the movie.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.760
And if you'd like more videos like this on
BoRap and other musicals, check out my full

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.810
playlist on the channel.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.369
Freddie Mercury's love of opera and its influence
on his work is well known.

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:28.619
So, it's fitting that the film includes several
references to this.

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:32.840
When Freddie proposes to Mary, for example,
in the background we hear the most famous

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.079
aria from Puccini's Madama Butterfly, a tragic
tale of the doomed marriage between an American

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.950
officer and a young Japanese girl.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.260
And the choice of this particular opera for
the film foreshadows the likewise ill-fated

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.370
nature of Freddie and Mary's marriage.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.489
Later, when Freddie rings Mary at night, we
hear an aria from another Puccini opera in

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:52.520
the background.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:57.590
The version we hear is sung by operatic soprano
Montserrat Caballé, with whom Freddie famously

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.050
recorded his 1988 album Barcelona.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:05.059
In a scene entirely made up for the movie,
Freddie more or less begs his former bandmates

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:09.319
to forgive him for breaking up the band and
asks them to play with him again at Live Aid.

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:11.520
'I need you &amp; you need me.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.610
Let's face it, we're not bad for four aging
queens.'

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:19.039
What's especially interesting here is that
Freddie's words 'we're not bad for four aging

00:01:19.039 --> 00:01:23.451
queens' are taken straight from the Magic
Tour in 1986 when, at Wembley Stadium, just

00:01:23.451 --> 00:01:27.369
before performing Who Wants To Live Forever,
Freddie addressed rumours that the band were

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:31.210
going to split up, telling the crowd that
they'd stay together until they died.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.520
'We're not bad for four aging queens, are
we?'

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.999
And there's another intriguing detail some
of you spotted in the scene where Freddie

00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:40.469
pleads with Brian, Roger, and John to re-form
the band.

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:45.020
After Freddie steps outside Miami's office
while the others talk among themselves, he

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.950
ends up standing right in front of a gold
record, giving the effect of a halo around

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.289
his head, which along with his conversation
with Miami neatly foreshadows his death.

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:55.479
'They'll be alright, they just need a bit
of time.'

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:56.789
'What if I don't have time?'

00:01:56.789 --> 00:02:01.130
On top of the 'four aging queens' line, there's
another hat-tip to the Magic Tour later during

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.429
Freddie's party when he descends the stairs
wearing a crown and full-length cloak.

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:11.039
At the end of shows during the band's 1986
tour, Freddie used to wear a custom-made jewel-covered

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.320
crown and a long red cloak while God Save
The Queen played.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.920
If you thought there was something familiar
about the embroidered top Freddie wears during

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.980
his first performance on stage with Brian
and Roger, then you'd be right as we actually

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:28.069
saw that exact same tunic being worn by Freddie's
mum at the start of the film.

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:32.019
Appropriately enough, just before Freddie
dons his mum's top, we see him trying on women's

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:36.349
clothes in the store where Mary works and
as she applies make-up to his face in the

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:40.239
dressing room, she tells him that she loves
his style and thinks we should all take more

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:41.290
risks.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.239
Just before the band meet their potential
new manager John Reid for the first time,

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:47.910
Brian comments on Freddie's jacket, telling
him it makes him look like "an angry lizard".

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.000
'I didn't know it was fancy dress Fred?'

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.640
'I've got to make an impression darling.'

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:51.719
'You look like an angry lizard.'

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:53.629
Which is interesting for two reasons.

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:58.590
First of all, bandmate John Deacon is played
by actor Joe Mazzello, who's best known for

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.700
his role as Tim in the dinosaur-sized blockbuster
Jurassic Park.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:07.950
And curiously, the term dinosaur comes from
Ancient Greek words meaning "terrible lizard".

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.000
But that's not the only fascinating detail
about this particular moment in Bohemian Rhapsody,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.280
as according to the costume designer, in real
life that same white leather jacket worn by

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.120
Rami Malek in the film "was found in Jimi
Hendrix's flat when he died", which makes

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.499
its appearance in the movie especially appropriate
as Hendrix was one of Freddie's favourite

00:03:25.499 --> 00:03:29.769
artists and an enormous influence on him,
as you can see from this photo of a young

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:31.569
Freddie posing as Hendrix.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:37.069
In fact, Hendrix also gets an amusingly ironic
shout-out during Mike Myers' cameo as fictional

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:41.939
EMI executive Ray Foster who refuses to release
'Bohemian Rhapsody'.

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:45.700
Foster makes a comparison between Freddie
and Jimi after the band lobs a brick through

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.159
Foster's window:
'You can take that out of our royalties!'

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:51.989
'You'll never have a gold disc, you medium
talent.'

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:55.370
'And to think, I worked with Hendrix.'

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.469
Shout-out to Joey C for pointing out that
Freddie's robe appears to be emblazoned with

00:03:59.469 --> 00:04:04.219
a dragon on the front and back, which could
be a sly wink to the Queen number Dragon Attack

00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:08.620
as the chorus for that song includes the line
"Gonna get me on the track / Got a dragon

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.019
on my back".

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:14.650
This shot of Freddie reflected in a pair of
aviator sunglasses as he nervously waits for

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.989
his former bandmates to arrive at Miami's
office for a possible reconciliation reminded

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:23.789
me of the shot of Freddie's sunglasses on
the back cover of his 1985 solo album, Mr

00:04:23.789 --> 00:04:28.300
Bad Guy, especially as the film veers from
reality by portraying Freddie as a sort of

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.680
"bad guy" for having broken up the band to
make a fortune with a solo deal.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.460
As Freddie sits at a bus stop, scribbling
on a loose piece of paper, we get a glimpse

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.690
of some lyrics from two different Queen tracks.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.990
First of all is the song Seven Seas of Rhye,
which featured on Queen's second album, Queen

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:51.240
II in 1974, after first appearing as an instrumental
on their self-titled debut album in 1973.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.419
Seven Seas of Rhye holds a special place in
Queen history as it was the first song they

00:04:55.419 --> 00:05:00.340
performed on the BBC's Top of the Pops as
well as their first entry in the UK charts

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:04.320
and its success convinced Freddie to dedicate
himself full-time to the band.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.640
The second set of lyrics we see on Freddie's
paper are for a song called Liar, which Freddie

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.729
wrote in 1970 and appeared on Queen's debut
album.

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:15.880
But those song lyrics aren't the only cool
little details in this scene.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.770
There's also an extra easter egg which you
might have missed.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:24.199
When Liar was released as a single in 1974,
its B-side was a track called "Doing Alright",

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:27.810
and you can hear that very song playing in
the background as Freddie sits waiting for

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.870
his bus.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:33.170
We hear "Doing Alright" again shortly later
when it's performed on stage by Brian and

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.850
Roger's earlier band Smile.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:39.470
The film tells us Freddie first met his future
Queen bandmates that night after Smile's lead

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:43.690
singer Tim Staffell quit the group, though,
as you might expect of a biopic, Bohemian

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:48.490
Rhapsody plays rather fast and loose with
the facts – more on that in my other video.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:52.550
What's interesting here, though, is that former
Smile frontman Tim Staffell actually got back

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:56.620
together with Brian and Roger especially for
the movie to re-record "Doing All Right",

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.720
the song he co-wrote with Brian; Freddie's
version of which appeared on Queen's debut

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:01.720
album.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.430
Thanks to the eagle-eyed people who told me
they spotted the teasmade that featured in

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:09.639
Queen's famous music video for I Want To Break
Free, which was a parody of the British soap

00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:11.449
opera Coronation Street.

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:16.030
The video's very British bedside tea-making
machine, which basically combines a kettle,

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:20.930
teapot, and alarm clock, appears in this scene
where Freddie's lounging on his bed.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:24.470
And there's another classic Queen reference
in this scene in the form of the band's logo

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.590
which you can see in Freddie's sketchbook.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:30.669
Originally, the film was also going to include
a scene with Freddie explaining how he came

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:34.940
up with the famous emblem, which, in addition
to the letter Q, a crown, and a Phoenix, is

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:40.319
made up of the band members' zodiac signs:
2 lions for Leos, Roger and John; a crab for

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:44.110
Brian's sign Cancer; and two fairies for Freddie's
Virgo.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.760
However, that scene ended up being deleted.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.240
By the way, if you're interested, I've got
a separate video on all the movie's deleted

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:51.880
scenes and songs.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.900
Several people also spotted Freddie singing
a couple of lines from the song Lazing On

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:59.590
A Sunday Afternoon from Queen's A Night At
The Opera album.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:04.930
As well as giving Fox's opening fanfare a
distinctive Queen touch, Brian May also lent

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:09.069
two suitcases full of his original clothes
from the era to the movie's costume designer

00:07:09.069 --> 00:07:13.590
so several of the outfits you see being worn
in the film are quite literally the genuine

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:14.930
article!

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:19.260
Cheers to everyone who pointed out that Brian
May's daughter, Emily, made a cameo here,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.600
and also that John Deacon's son, Luke, had
a cameo in this scene too!

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.120
In a previous video, I pointed out Bono's
cameo and Dire Straits music playing in the

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.120
Live Aid scenes.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.659
Well, they're not the only artists who get
a shout-out in the film.

00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:36.470
David Bowie and Elton John, or rather two
actors made up to look like them, get a couple

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.350
of sneaky cameos.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:41.680
The first time we see them is at the start
of the movie, though David's face is hidden

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.361
by a camera and Elton's is only seen as a
reflection.

00:07:45.361 --> 00:07:49.199
However, we see them both again, very briefly,
at the end of the film, as Queen head towards

00:07:49.199 --> 00:07:51.030
the Live Aid stage.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:55.590
And right at the very start of the film, there's
a few seconds of real Live Aid footage playing

00:07:55.590 --> 00:08:00.169
in one of the TV trucks, where you can see
Princess Diana, Prince Charles, Bob Geldof,

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:04.210
Elton John, George Michael, as well as Brian
May and Roger Taylor!

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.580
Current-day Brian May also gets a cameo in
the movie though he's in disguise and we don't

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:10.550
see his face in the final cut.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:14.300
However, if you look carefully at the Top
of the Pops sequence, this is Brian reading

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.250
a British tabloid.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:21.270
And tons of you think Brian and Roger Taylor
also appeared on the Live Aid set, sitting

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:22.880
on the scaffold watching the concert.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:27.340
Now, it could be them, but if it is they're
quite heavily disguised.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.340
What do you think?

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.099
Let me know in the comments below.

00:08:30.099 --> 00:08:33.590
There's even more cool easter eggs you may
have missed in Bohemian Rhapsody including

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:38.820
secret references to Fat Bottomed Girls, Wayne's
World, Marlene Dietrich, and an Adam Lambert

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:39.820
cameo.

